+
+# JSON Patch: strict validation of patch and values, every failure a JsonPatchError
+
+## Context
+
+Users of `applyPatch` (task-002, semantics proposed in dl-003, still pending) reported invalid patches
+being accepted and failures not behaving as RFC 6902 reads. A review found that the JSON data model
+of RFC 8259, which RFC 6902 sections 3 and 4 rely on ("a JSON Patch document is a JSON document that
+represents an array of objects"), was not enforced at the JavaScript boundary:
+
+- `patch.forEach` skipped the holes of a sparse patch, so `[, op]` applied `op` silently;
+- class instances, and objects whose `op` was inherited, were accepted as operation objects;
+- sparse arrays were copied with their holes (read back as `undefined`, written out as `null`);
+- a cyclic value, a throwing getter or proxy, or a value too deep for the call stack escaped as a
+  raw `RangeError`/`TypeError`, so the caller saw no `JsonPatchError`, no `index`, no `operation`.
+
+## Decision
+
+- Iterate the patch by index: a hole is an operation that is not an object, so the patch is invalid.
+- An operation must be a plain object (prototype `Object.prototype` or `null`); its `op` is read only
+  as an own member, like `path`, `from` and `value` already were.
+- A JSON value contains no hole: a sparse array, in the document or a `value`, is not a JSON value.
+- Cycles are detected while copying (tracking the containers on the current path, so an object that
+  merely appears twice is still accepted and copied twice) and reported as not a JSON value.
+- `applyPatch` throws nothing but `JsonPatchError`: any error raised while applying an operation is
+  wrapped with that operation's `index` and `operation`, the original error as `cause` (still the
+  `JsonPointerError` when a pointer is at fault).
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Section 5 ("if a normative requirement is violated ... application of the entire patch document
+  SHALL NOT be deemed successful") now holds for every malformed input, and callers can rely on a
+  single error type to locate the failing operation.
+- Patches built as class instances are now rejected; callers must pass plain objects (as parsed JSON is).
+- Valid but extremely deep documents (beyond the call stack) still fail, now as a `JsonPatchError`
+  whose cause is the `RangeError`; an iterative copy would lift this limit if ever needed.
+- Unchanged from dl-003: non-enumerable and symbol-keyed properties are ignored, as `JSON.stringify` does.
